 # Generator member calls are grouped — `(r.string …)`, `(r.int …)`: the bare
 # form declines its compile stamp and runs on the interpreter (boru
-# COMPILABLE-SUBSET §5, boru-lang/boru#528). A list-of generator (a member
-# call taking an inner generator body) lives in `key-list`, called grouped,
-# because inline it either declines or, grouped inline, loses `r`; the call
-# inside `key-list` must stay grouped too (bare, the generators calling it
-# decline again). `r.list-of` still runs its element body on the interpreter,
-# once per key (DX-REPORT). Same draws, same order, same values per seed.
+# COMPILABLE-SUBSET §5, boru-lang/boru#528). A list generator lives in
+# `key-list`, called grouped from each generator.
+#
+# `key-list` draws the count, then collects one key per `for` iteration. It
+# does not call `r.list-of [ r.string … ] (r.int lo hi)`: that native runs its
+# element body on a pooled interpreter, once per key, even from compiled code
+# (and inline it either declines or, grouped inline, loses `r`). The draws
+# are the same ones in the same order — count first, then for each key its
+# length and its characters — so every seed generates the same lists
+# (DX-REPORT, "Run-time interpreter entries"). The count's def is typed:
+# untyped, `for` cannot dispatch on it at compile time and every generator
+# calling `key-list` declines its stamp ("unmatched dispatch recovered at
+# for").
 
 # A list of (r.int lo hi) random keys, each 1..maxlen chars from `charset`.
 # Call: `(key-list maxlen lo hi r)`.
 def key-list fn [
   [maxlen:Integer lo:Integer hi:Integer r:Map] [List] [
-    (r.list-of [ r.string charset (r.int 1 maxlen) ] (r.int lo hi))
+    def n:Integer (r.int lo hi)
+    [for n [ (r.string charset (r.int 1 maxlen)) ]]
   ]
 ]
 
@@ -142,7 +150,14 @@ def specs [
 ]
 
 
-def _ (specs each [ var [[s] (s Test.run-property end) report 0 ] ])
+# The loop variable is `spec`, a name nothing on the properties' call paths
+# binds. As `s` it was live at top level while each property ran, and the
+# fns the properties call bind `s` too (so do `k`, `acc`, `ks`, `x`, `p`).
+# With a Reach lens (`each $.1`) also on the path, boru found the stamped
+# property body stale at every run, and after three re-stamps the rest of
+# its runs took the interpreter (radix and tst: 96 runs each; DX-REPORT,
+# "Run-time interpreter entries").
+def _ (specs each [ var [[spec] (spec Test.run-property end) report 0 ] ])
 
 
